3. Other prior work credited for interoperability
-------------------------------------------------

  * The text-generation models whose weights this software can load (for example
    BitNet b1.58 2B4T and Falcon3) are products of their publishers and are distributed under
    their own licenses. No model weights are part of this software.
  * Packing five ternary digits into one byte as a base-3 fraction is also used by the TQ1_0
    format of ggml (section 1). The velurex encoding used by Quintrit, with the digit
    weights 1, 3, 9, 28 and 84 and the tiled layout of the kernels, is a different encoding.
  * The test helpers use the byte-to-unicode mapping of the byte-level BPE vocabulary
    introduced with GPT-2 (OpenAI, MIT license) to synthesize test vocabularies.

The names BitNet, Microsoft, Falcon, LLaMA, Hugging Face, GGUF and GGML are used only to
identify the formats and models this software is compatible with. Quintrit is not affiliated
with, endorsed by or sponsored by their owners.


4. Third-party software libraries
---------------------------------

Quintrit depends on open-source Rust crates, among them `tokenizers` (Hugging Face), `rayon`,
`serde`, `serde_json`, `ahash` and, for the Python bindings, `pyo3` and `numpy`. They are
not part of this repository; each is used under its own license (MIT, Apache-2.0 or a
combination of permissive licenses). Binary distributions (Python wheels,
shared and static libraries) contain the compiled code of these crates; their license texts
can be listed with `cargo about` or `cargo license` from the locked dependency set
(`Cargo.lock`).
